# L §8 What a result reads (tally 2.1.7 R1, issue #140; #116, #117): over things `first` and `last` run in the order
# of their `ties by` term (L §5.11 l.1170), so every thing's term is read first, a later term only among the things
# the earlier ones leave tied, and then the `where`s in that order, stopping at the first true one. t1: bob is first
# by born and his `where` is true, so cid's and ana's `where`s (a division by zero) are never read. t3: ana is least
# by rank alone, so sub[ana], a hole, is never read; t4: bob and cid tie on rank, and sub breaks it. 2.1.6's refcore
# read every `where` (RUN-DIV) and every term of every thing (the hole of sub[ana]).
=== m.tally
model fx
  tally 2
  on 2026-03-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source card "invented"

kind persons

input base: int

input born[p in persons]: date

input rank[p in persons]: int

input sub[p in persons]: int

input gate[p in persons]: int

ask t1: first(p in persons on 2026-02-01 where p == bob or base / gate[p] > 0) ties by born[p]

ask t2: last(p in persons on 2026-02-01 where p == ana or base / gate[p] > 0) ties by born[p]

ask t3: first(p in persons on 2026-02-01) ties by (rank[p], sub[p])

ask t4: last(p in persons on 2026-02-01) ties by (rank[p], sub[p])

ask t5: first(p in persons on 2026-02-01 where rank[p] > 1) ties by (rank[p], sub[p])

version v1
  cite card
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  persons + [ana] lives 2026-01-01..
  persons + [bob] lives 2026-01-01..
  persons + [cid] lives 2026-01-01..
  base = 6 "c"
  born[ana] = 2000-01-01 "x"
  born[bob] = 1990-01-01 "x"
  born[cid] = 1995-01-01 "x"
  rank[ana] = 1 "x"
  rank[bob] = 2 "x"
  rank[cid] = 2 "x"
  sub[bob] = 5 "x"
  sub[cid] = 4 "x"
  gate[ana] = 0 "x"
  gate[bob] = 0 "x"
  gate[cid] = 0 "x"
=== expect t1 t2 t3 t4 t5
t1: bob
  cut 2026-03-01
t2: ana
  cut 2026-03-01
t3: ana
  cut 2026-03-01
t4: bob
  cut 2026-03-01
t5: cid
  cut 2026-03-01
